			================================
			Credits for predict-ng
			================================

predict-ng is a modernized continuation of PREDICT, originally written
by John A. Magliacane, KD2BD <kd2bd@amsat.org>.

predict-ng modernization (v3.0) by Costin Stroie, YO3JAZ
<yo3jaz@eridu.eu.org>, May 2026:
  - Replaced installer.c with a standard ./configure + Makefile build
  - Eliminated generated predict.h in favour of compile-time -D macros
  - Changed longitude convention to East-positive (standard)
  - Rewrote kepupdate for current Celestrak GP API
  - Added SIGHUP live reload of orbital data
  - Integrated vocalizer fully into the build system
  - Updated man page, README, AUTHORS, CHANGES


			================================
			Credits for the original PREDICT
			================================

SGP4/SDP4 models and sun/moon position algorithms were derived from
"satcom-08" written by Neoklis Kyriazis, 5B4AZ, released in 2002 under
the GNU General Public License.  Parts of "satcom-08" were derived
through a translation into C of SGP4 and SDP4 code written originally
in FORTRAN and Pascal by Dr. T.S. Kelso.  Dr. Kelso's code is available
at https://celestrak.org/.

Socket-based server code and the "map" client application were
contributed by Ivan Galysh, KD4HBO on 10-Jan-2000.

PIC/TRACK antenna rotator control code was contributed by
Vittorio Benvenuti, I3VFJ on 13-Jul-2000.

The "gsat" client was contributed by Xavier Crehueras, EB3CZS.

The Perl client demonstration program was contributed by Rich
Parry, W9IF.

The WinPredictClient was contributed by Steve Fraser, VK5ASF.

The concept of using "xearth" as a graphical display for PREDICT
was originally conceived by Wade Hampton (later developed into
"earthtrack").

Calculations for squint angle were adopted from trk-0.4.1 by Lapo
Pieri, IK5NAX.

The QuickDoppler feature was contributed by Joeal Subash.

Many others have contributed code, bug reports, bug fixes, and
suggestions, including Bent Bagger OZ6BL, Tom Busch WB8WOR,
Steve Fraser VK5ASF, Xavier Crehueras EB3CZS, and Takeshi Okazaki
(Satellite Engineer for MTSAT-1R / HIMAWARI 6).


